msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:3
#: manual/index.md:20
msgid "Fast buyer check"
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:5
msgid "**Problem.** When the game decides whether the customer will buy an item (`StoreClient.IsClientBuyingThisItem`), it also checks a container's contents. It lists every item anywhere inside, runs the full check on each one, and each of those lists and checks its own contents again. For nested storage that is quadratic, and every level allocates a new list."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:7
msgid "The game runs this check when an item is placed on the counter, when it decides whether something can be sold, and in the negotiation screen, once for every item in the back inventory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:9
msgid "**Fix.** The rule, read from the game's code:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:11
msgid "an item is **bought** when it is acceptable on its own (the game's own check with `ignoreContents`) and, if its contents are checked, everything inside it is bought;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:12
msgid "contents are **checked** unless the item is currently locked, has no content window, or is an advanced gun."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:14
msgid "`IsClientBuyingThisItem` and `IsClientRefusingItem`, in the forms that include contents, are patched to evaluate exactly that rule bottom-up, once per item per call. Answers are reused only within that call, never across calls or frames, so the result is always the game's own for the current state. The single-item form runs unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:16
msgid "Contents nested more than 32 levels deep get no answer from the rule. For such a call the game's own check runs instead, and its checks of the items inside come back through the fix one level down each time, so even then the answer is the game's."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:18
msgid "**When it starts.** The game's own check uses a list of localized water names that the game fills in the first time it is needed. Installing the fix fills it in too, and mods start before the game applies the player's language. So the fix waits until the language the game chose at startup is applied, and only then installs. If that has not happened within five minutes, the fix stays off for the session and `Player.log` says so. Without this wait, water names would stay in English for the session and water would stop selling in any other language."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:1
#: manual/index.md:21
#: manual/index.md:22
msgid "Diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:3
msgid "Long-frame log"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:5
msgid "With **Log long frames** on, frames longer than 100 ms are written to the game's `Player.log` as `[PerformanceFixes] long frame: ...`, with the container window opened just before, if any, and counters for each fix. It costs nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:7
msgid "`Player.log` is in `%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\LocalLow\\Questing Goose Studio\\Probably Stolen`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:9
msgid "Long-frame profiler"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:11
msgid "**Profile long frames** goes further. While it is on, every per-frame callback of the game's MonoBehaviours, every layout pass (`Validate`) and everything the shop's main update calls, four levels deep, is timed, along with Unity's UI rebuild and rendering. Each frame over 100 ms is then logged with its slowest methods:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:17
msgid "Times are inclusive: a method that calls others includes them. The profiler adds a little overhead, so leave it off for normal play. It never lets a failure reach the game: if it cannot attach to a method, it skips it, and if it cannot start, it removes everything it attached."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:19
msgid "When a fix cannot attach"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:21
msgid "After a game update, a fix may no longer find a method it patches. It then removes the patches it had already attached, so it is either fully on or fully off, and writes one line to `Player.log`: `[PerformanceFixes] <fix> is off: ...`. The other fixes keep working."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:23
msgid "Regenerating the method lists"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:25
msgid "The timed methods are listed in generated files, so the game's mod scanner sees every type named in code and asks for no permission:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:27
msgid "`tools/GenProbe.cs` writes `src/Targets.g.cs`, the per-frame callbacks and layout passes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:28
msgid "`tools/GenCallees.cs` writes `src/Callees.g.cs`, everything the given methods call, to a given depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/diagnostics.md:30
msgid "Both leave out the game's mod loader, mod security and Steam integration, which mods may not patch. Regenerate them after a game update."
msgstr ""

