#: manual/inventories.md:5
msgid "**Problem.** `GameItem.ValidateShapeState` runs whenever an item enters or leaves an inventory and on every tag change (`EnableTag`, `DisableTag`, `ModifyTag`). After laying out the item, it calls `Validate` on the item's whole inventory, which lays out every item in it again. Adding n items costs n × n layouts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:7
msgid "One save load in a large store ran 161,804 item layouts and 7,000 inventory layouts in a single frame, about 1.8 s. A single tag change on an item in a full storage bay re-lays out the whole bay, and opening a container is one such tag change (`OPENED_TAG`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:9
msgid "**Fix.** While `ValidateShapeState` runs, the inventory layouts it asks for are only recorded. The item's own layout, and everything else, still happens immediately. The recorded inventories are laid out once, just before Unity renders the frame (`Canvas.willRenderCanvases`). What reaches the screen is the same, but repeated requests in one frame cost one layout."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:11
msgid "An inventory laid out directly in the meantime is dropped from the list, and an inventory whose UI was destroyed since is skipped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:15
msgid "**Problem.** While items are selected, and all the while they are dragged as a group, the game redraws the highlight under them every frame. It switches off every highlight square it lit the frame before and then lights the squares under each selected item again. Each square that goes off and on is rebuilt and its canvas re-batched, every frame. To find each item's slot, the game also looks the item up in a fresh copy of its inventory's item list, so n selected items in an inventory of m items copy n × m entries per frame. A large selection in a full storage bay stutters the whole time it is held or dragged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:17
msgid "Over the items' own container, a group drag is always a precise placement (elsewhere only with Shift held), and the game also redraws the placement preview every frame in the same way. To colour the preview, it checks every selected item against every item in the container, and for each of those it searches the list of selected items, which is n × m × n steps per frame. This is why dragging inside the original container stuttered most."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:19
msgid "**Fix.** The same squares are lit in the same way, and then only the squares lit the frame before that are not lit now are switched off. A square that stays lit is not touched, so the highlight looks exactly as before and an unchanged selection costs almost nothing. Each item's slot is kept between frames while the item stays in the same inventory with the same shape, so the item list is no longer copied every frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:21
msgid "The placement preview is updated in place in the same way. Whether the group fits is worked out again only when the drop position, the selection, or an item in any inventory changes. Every other condition is still checked each frame, and the drop itself still runs the game's own check."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:25
msgid "**Problem.** A save is decoded in eleven parts, one per inventory. Each part builds its items, restores their tags, runs about fifteen load steps on every item (machines, locks, plants, water sprites and more) and moves the items into the inventory. Every tag change and every move recomputes the item's shape and state and then redraws the item's look; redrawing an item also redraws every container it is inside, and every inventory layout redraws all its items again. In a store of 1,342 items that came to about 58,000 shape checks and 62,000 redraws, over a second of the load: each item's look worked out about 45 times."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:27
msgid "**Fix.** The redraws of one part are combined into a single pass, at the end of that same part of the load: each item that asked to be redrawn is redrawn once, in the order it first asked, then the containers around it once each; items destroyed during the part are skipped. Everything else runs exactly where and as often as in the game: shapes and states, placement, inventory layouts (they also set sizes and slot positions that later shape computations read) and every load step, none of which reads an item's look. An item's look is worked out from its current shape, state and contents, so the one pass gives what the game's last redraw gives."
msgstr ""

#: manual/inventories.md:29
msgid "Each load writes one line to `Player.log`, with or without the option: `[PerformanceFixes] save load: ... ms`, the number of redraws asked for and run, and the number of items in each part's inventory. Loading the same save with the option on and off shows the time saved and that the same items were loaded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:3
msgid "Four fixes make opening and closing full storage windows smooth. They were found with the profiler: the storage-preset hotkey, which opens four full windows at once, froze the game for about 300 ms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:7
msgid "**Problem.** The game puts every floating window under one canvas, `FloatingWindowCanvas`. Unity rebuilds a canvas as a whole: whenever anything in it changes, every graphic in every open window is re-batched. A window opening, an item moving or a slot lighting up under the mouse is enough. With a few large storage windows open, that is thousands of graphics per change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:9
msgid "**Fix.** Before a container window is shown, it gets a nested canvas, so a change inside it rebuilds only that window. The nested canvas keeps the parent's draw order, copies the root's reference pixels-per-unit and shader channels so sprites and text look the same, and has its own raycaster so its contents still take clicks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:11
msgid "The game finds window bounds through `Canvas.rootCanvas` and positions windows with its own window canvas, so placement and dragging are unaffected. Tooltips and menus are left alone."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:15
msgid "**Problem.** When a window opens, the game moves it from its home, the scene's window canvas, under the floating-window canvas (`UIFamilyLayer.Adopt`). When it closes, the game moves it back (`ReleaseWindow`). Moving a hierarchy between canvases makes every graphic and text in it re-register and rebuild, so a full storage window costs tens of milliseconds each way. This was about 140 ms of the 280 ms preset stutter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:17
msgid "**Fix.** A closed window stays under the floating canvas. The next time it opens, the game finds it already in place and only brings it to the front. A window is kept only when its home is in the same scene as the floating canvas, so a scene change destroys it together with its home, exactly as before. A window being destroyed goes home first as usual."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:21
msgid "**Problem.** Hiding a window deactivates its GameObject, and showing it activates it again. For a full storage window that means thousands of components switched off and on again, about 110 ms for one large window each way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:23
msgid "**Fix.** Switching off the window's own canvas hides it just as well and costs almost nothing: nothing is drawn, and its raycaster is off, so it takes no clicks. The game reads a window's state only through `PixelWindow.IsVisible` and changes it only in `Show` and `Hide`, so for container windows that have their own canvas and stay in place:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:25
msgid "`Hide` performs the game's own steps in the same order: it remembers the window position, releases the window and removes it from the open-window list. The only difference is that it switches the canvas off instead of deactivating the object."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:26
msgid "`Show` switches the canvas back on first. After that, the game's `SetActive(true)` finds the object already active and does nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:27
msgid "`IsVisible` answers false for a window hidden this way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:29
msgid "Each window still pays the full cost the first time it opens. Turning the option off restores every such window to an ordinary, deactivated one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:33
msgid "**Problem.** The storage-preset hotkey opens or closes every window of the preset in one frame, so any remaining cost freezes every animation for its whole length."
msgstr ""

#: manual/windows.md:35
msgid "**Fix.** The first window opens or closes at once, so the key answers immediately, and the others follow on the next frames, one each. Each window's tab selection is applied right after its own opening, as the game does. Pressing the hotkey again while some are still pending finishes them first, so the game always sees a settled state."
msgstr ""
